CORE
main.cpp
--cpp98
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ initializer: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ member access: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [expr.cond]/1, /7: `b ? D(1) : D(2)' evaluates one operand; the
result is a prvalue initialized from it.  The front end materialized both
prvalues as temporaries and selected between them, so both constructors
ran (bin53).  The conditional is now one temporary whose initializer
initializes it from the selected operand.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept
this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
